// Matching without materialising a substring.
//
// str_eqat is the primitive the rest of the scanning half is written on: it
// answers "does txt appear in s starting at i" without allocating anything,
// which is what makes a keyword test free. The return clause reads as an
// assignment, but `=` in an expression is equality, so it yields 1 only when
// every byte matched.
//
// **It alternates charat between two strings**, which misses the runtime's
// one-entry length memo on every call. Measured in idem: 200 000 of these
// against a 131 KB source cost 167 ms for that reason alone. Right once per
// line; wrong inside a per-token loop, where comparing byte codes directly is
// what a lexer should do.
//
// str_eqat lifted from idem/engine/core/util/str.id.

str_eqat(string s, int i, string txt) {
  int j = 0;
  while (j < len(txt) && charat(s, i + j) = charat(txt, j)) {
    j = j + 1;
  }
} return int j = len(txt);

// Does s begin with txt? An empty txt matches anything, which is what makes
// str_starts(s, "") consistent with str_find(s, "") = 0.
str_starts(string s, string txt) {
} return int str_eqat(s, 0, txt);

// Does s end with txt? The guard is not cosmetic: a txt longer than s gives a
// negative index, and charat of a negative index is not the end sentinel.
str_ends(string s, string txt) {
  int i = len(s) - len(txt);
} return int (i >= 0) && str_eqat(s, i, txt);
